{"id":"h048","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"The atomic radius of hydrogen is 0.037 nm, Compare this figure with the length of the first Bohr radius. Explain any differences.\nIs the correct answer \"The atomic radius of hydrogen is smaller than the first Bohr radius\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The statement that hydrogen's atomic radius is smaller than the first Bohr radius is correct.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.1566},{"text":"0.037 nm (37 pm) is numerically smaller than the Bohr radius a₀ ≈ 0.0529 nm.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.1454},{"text":"The numerical comparison decides the matter.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-0.0512},{"text":"The two quantities are defined differently, so the comparison is not like-for-like.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.0599},{"text":"The 0.037 nm value is an empirical covalent radius, about half the H–H bond length (74 pm) in H₂.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.2365},{"text":"The Bohr radius is a theoretical quantity, the lowest orbit radius and the most probable distance of the 1s electron.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1123},{"text":"Because the definitions differ, the comparison might be argued to be not meaningful, but this does not change the outcome.","confidence":0.15,"lean":-0.5,"theta":0.2356,"phi":1.2767},{"text":"In H₂ bonding shares electron density between two nuclei and pulls the cloud inward, so half the internuclear distance is smaller than the isolated-atom Bohr radius.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.2197},{"text":"The expectation value ⟨r⟩ for 1s is 1.5a₀ (about 79 pm), so size depends on the measure used.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.2,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-2.9639}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.7,"r